course: everyday
lesson: 379
title: Maddie's nose and Connor's «equality»
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Maddie had shoved Connor in the line, and Connor punched her in the face. The next morning she looked in the bathroom mirror at a nose that was swollen, purple and twice its size. «I look like a plum,» she said, and then she cried, which she hadn't done in front of anyone the day before.
explain: At school Connor told his friends, «I hit her because equality. She wants to fight like a boy, so she gets hit like a boy.» Some of the class agreed: «Maddie deserved it, she's always shoving.» When the school called Connor's dad in, he said, «Well, she was asking for it.» That evening Maddie's big brother Ryan wanted to «sort Connor out» after school. Mum said no, flatly.
explain: Mum sat on the end of Maddie's bed and asked a hard question: «Do you want to fight like the boys, or do you want to be treated fairly?» Maddie thought for a long time. Equality, Mum said, means everyone gets the same respect and the same rules — and the rule is that nobody hits anybody. It doesn't mean «now I'm allowed to punch you». A punch to the face is wrong whoever throws it and whoever gets it.
explain: That night Maddie wrote in her diary: «Connor was wrong to hit me. I was wrong to shove him. I'm still angry.» All three sentences were true at once. A week later Mrs Okafor put them in the same group for science. They didn't speak for ten minutes, then argued about magnets, and by the end Connor muttered «sorry about your nose». Sam was the only one who had thought to ask her whether it hurt.
fact: Equality means everyone gets the same respect and the same rules, not that anyone may hit anyone.
fact: Hitting someone in the face is wrong whether the person is a boy or a girl.
fact: Being shoved is wrong too, but a punch to the face is a much bigger harm than a shove.
fact: «She was asking for it» is an excuse; nobody deserves to be hurt.
fact: Two people can both be in the wrong, and one can still be more in the wrong.
fact: A big brother «sorting someone out» is revenge and usually makes things worse for everyone.
fact: A hit to the nose can cause swelling and bruising that lasts a week or more, and sometimes a broken nose.
fact: You can admit your own part in a fight and still be angry about what was done to you.
fact: Working together on something new, like a science task, often helps two people who fell out.
fact: Asking «does it hurt?» is a small kindness that people remember for a long time.
exercise: What did Maddie do first that started the trouble? | She shoved Connor in the line | She punched Connor hard on the arm | She called Connor rude names in the line | She hid Connor's bag in the bin
exercise: What did Maddie see in the mirror the next morning? | A swollen, purple nose | A tiny scratch on the very tip | Nothing at all, it looked normal | A black eye but no swelling
exercise: Why did Maddie cry the next morning, not the day before? | She hadn't wanted anyone to see her cry | Her nose only started to hurt in the morning | She was upset about missing her breakfast | She had lost her diary on the bus
exercise: What did Connor say equality meant? | That he could hit her like a boy | That everyone gets the same respect at school | That girls must never play rough with boys | That boys and girls should sit apart
exercise: What does equality really mean? | Same respect, same rules, and nobody hits anybody | Everyone is allowed to hit everyone the same amount | Girls must be treated more gently than boys, always | Boys must always let the girls win at every game
exercise: Some of the class said «Maddie deserved it». What's wrong with that? | Nobody deserves to be punched in the face | Maddie never shoved anyone at all | They should say Connor deserved it instead | Nothing, they were completely right
exercise: What was wrong with Connor's dad saying «she was asking for it»? | It excused his son hurting someone | He said it far too quietly to be heard | He should have shouted at Maddie instead | Nothing, it was a perfectly fair point
exercise: Why did Mum say no to Ryan «sorting Connor out»? | Revenge would make things worse for everyone | Ryan was far too small to win a fight with Connor | Connor's dad was watching by the school gate | Ryan had his homework to finish first
exercise: Which of Maddie's diary sentences was true? | All three: Connor was wrong, she was wrong, she's angry | Only the line saying that Connor was the one wrong | Only the line saying that she was the one in the wrong | None of them, she was making the whole thing up
exercise: Who was more in the wrong, and why? | Connor, because a punch to the face is far worse than a shove | Maddie, because she was the one who shoved first in the line | Both exactly the same, because a shove is just as bad | Neither, because it was all just play-fighting
exercise: What did Mum's question «fight like the boys or be treated fairly» help Maddie see? | That fairness means no hitting, not hitting like boys | That she should learn to punch much harder than the boys do | That girls should never ever get angry | That boys are always right in a fight
exercise: How did science group help Maddie and Connor? | Arguing about magnets got them talking again | The teacher made them hug in front of the class | They had to fight it out in the playground | They sat back to back and never spoke again
exercise: How do you think Maddie felt when Connor muttered «sorry about your nose»? | A bit better, though still sore and angry | Furious that he even dared to speak to her | Nothing at all, she didn't even hear what he said | Glad, deep down, that she had got punched after all
exercise: Why did Sam's question matter? | He was the only one who asked how she felt | He wanted to start a fight with Connor after school | He was secretly laughing at her swollen nose | He was training to be a doctor
exercise: What brave thing could Connor have done instead of boasting? | Admit he was wrong and say sorry straight away | Hit someone else just to prove that he was tough enough | Hide in the toilets for the whole day | Blame his dad for everything he did
exercise: What brave thing did Maddie do in her diary? | Admitted her own part while still angry | Planned exactly how she would get Connor back next week | Wrote that she never shoves anybody | Tore out the whole page about Connor
test: «Connor says it's equality, she wants to fight like a boy so she gets hit like one». Is that what equality means? | No, it means same respect and nobody hits anybody | Yes, that's exactly what equality means, fair's fair | Yes, but only for girls who are tomboys | No, it means girls are allowed to hit boys
test: «tbh maddie had it coming, always shoving people». Which is true? | Shoving is wrong, but nobody deserves a punch in the face | She totally deserved it for always shoving people in the line | Shoving is fine, so only Connor did wrong | It doesn't matter, it was only her nose
test: Ryan: «I'm gonna sort that Connor kid out after school». What's wisest? | Don't, revenge makes it worse; let the school deal with it | Do it, somebody has to stand up to him | Do it, but only on the way home so the teachers don't see | Get his mates to do it so he doesn't get caught
test: «her nose was like a massive plum lmao». Is it a joke to Maddie? | No, it hurt and she cried about it | Yes, she loved it | Yes, plums are funny | No, because it didn't swell at all
test: «Connor's dad said she was asking for it, so it's fine then». Is it? | No, it's an excuse, not a reason | Yes, because dads always know best | Yes, if a grown-up says so it's true | Only if she actually asked out loud
test: «if they're both wrong then they're just as bad». Is that right? | No, both can be wrong while one is much more wrong | Yes, wrong is wrong, so they're both exactly as bad as each other | Yes, so nobody should have to say sorry | No, only Connor did anything wrong at all
test: «why'd Mrs O put them in the same science group, she's mental». Why might she have done it? | Working on something together can help them make up | She wanted to see another fight between the two of them | She forgot all about the punch in the line | She was really trying to punish Sam
test: Maddie: «I'm still angry but I know I shoved him too». Can both be true? | Yes, you can own your part and still be angry | No, if you were the one who shoved first you can't be angry | No, angry people can never admit anything | Only if Connor says sorry to her first
test: «boys shouldn't hit girls, but girls can hit boys». Is that fair? | No, nobody should hit anybody | Yes, that's what equality means | Yes, boys are tougher | Only on Fridays
test: «Sam asked if her nose hurt, so soppy». Was it soppy? | No, it was a small kindness she remembered | Yes, he should have laughed along with the rest | Yes, it was none of his business at all | No, he was only asking because he was bored
test: «a punch on the nose, it's nothing, it'll be fine tomorrow». True? | No, swelling and bruises can last a week or more | Yes, noses heal overnight while you sleep | Yes, because a nose is too soft to really hurt | No, a broken nose never heals at all
test: «maddie's a tomboy, so she's not really a girl, so it's ok». What's wrong with this? | She is a girl, and nobody should be hit anyway | Nothing, it's a perfectly fair point when you think about it | Tomboys are allowed to be hit like boys | It's only wrong if she ends up crying
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle of equality as it pertains to physical conduct between pupils? | Every pupil is owed equal respect and is bound by the rule that none may strike another | Every pupil is equally entitled to strike any other pupil who has first provoked them | Female pupils are exempt from all rules of conduct governing physical contact | Male pupils may strike female pupils who have previously shoved them in line
exam: Identify the principal deficiency in the assertion that a person who was struck «was asking for it». | It excuses harm, whereas no person deserves to be injured | It is expressed with insufficient formality for a school setting | It ought properly to be addressed to the victim | It is entirely without deficiency
exam: Given that pupil A shoved pupil B and pupil B subsequently struck pupil A in the face, determine which statement is consistent with the established facts. | Both acted wrongly, the blow to the face constituting the greater harm | Pupil A alone acted wrongly, having acted first | Pupil B alone acted wrongly, the shove being permissible | Neither acted wrongly, the exchange being reciprocal
exam: Ascertain the most probable consequence of a sibling undertaking to «sort out» a pupil who has injured a family member. | An escalation of the conflict to the detriment of all concerned | A permanent and amicable resolution between the two families | Commendation by the school authorities | The immediate healing of the original injury
